 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Iso-Dist UI Interaction
-A specific interaction mode where the transfer function is warped into non-uniform data space based on bin density [S3, unofficial]. It displays bin boundaries as black lines to show non-uniformness and denseness [S3, unofficial]. The color map is compressed in high-density areas and stretched in wide bins [S3, unofficial]. The opacity function is also warped into this non-uniform space [S3, unofficial]. This mode is chosen when dealing with high dynamic range data sets where uniform sampling is insufficient, but it requires careful handling of histogram orientation since the linear axis is maintained for user familiarity [S3, unofficial].
+A variant that warps opacity and color functions into non-uniform data space (Iso-Dist mode) to handle high dynamic range data [S4, unofficial]. It displays bin boundaries, uses a logarithmic opacity scale, and allows zooming/panning for detail transition [S4, unofficial]. Choose this when dealing with non-uniform data distributions where linear mapping flattens the histogram [S4, unofficial].
 
 ### Transfer Function History Tree
-A persistent history tree of transfer functions used to produce visualizations, allowing retrieval of previous versions and reproduction of results [S4, unofficial]. The user saves the current transfer function in the second level of interaction, storing it as a tree node together with a snapshot of the rendered volume [S4, unofficial]. The history tree is displayed in a separate window, with each node graphically represented by its snapshot [S4, unofficial]. Any saved transfer function can be retrieved by clicking on the respective tree node [S4, unofficial]. The tree can be scaled and translated by dragging the mouse, with automatic layout maintenance [S4, unofficial]. The user can delete branches of the tree when it becomes too large [S4, unofficial].
+A persistent history tree that stores TFs and rendered snapshots as nodes [S5, unofficial]. It allows retrieval, scaling, translation, and branch deletion [S5, unofficial]. Choose this when reproducibility and iterative exploration of multiple TF configurations are required [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The computation time scales with the number of sources (nodes) [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of control points and sources. For the Mixture Graph integration, CPU performance is limited by the number of nodes, while GPU performance is limited by synchronization [S2].
 *   **Measured Performance:**
-    *   **Hippocampus (353 sources, depth 11):** 22.7ms on CPU, 3.5ms on GPU [S2].
-    *   **Neocortex (1,182 sources, depth 22):** 129.7ms on CPU, 11.3ms on GPU [S2].
-    *   **Polymer (15,917 sources, depth 26):** 57.0ms on CPU, 12.1ms on GPU [S2].
-*   **Scaling:** The GPU performance remains relatively stable across increasing source counts (3.5ms to 12.1ms), while CPU performance varies more significantly (22.7ms to 129.7ms), indicating different bottlenecks [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables interactive multidimensional transfer function editing; supports fast transition between levels of detail via zoom/pan; allows integration into various workflows (e.g., neuroscientists classifying neural structures, material scientists clustering fibers) [S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** CPU performance is limited by the number of nodes; GPU performance is limited by synchronization overhead [S2].
+    *   Hippocampus (353 sources, depth 11): 22.7ms CPU, 3.5ms GPU [S2].
+    *   Neocortex (1,182 sources, depth 22): 129.7ms CPU, 11.3ms GPU [S2].
+    *   Polymer (15,917 sources, depth 26): 57.0ms CPU, 12.1ms GPU [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of control points and the size of the transfer function texture [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Interactive, real-time feedback; supports complex structural cut-aways; persistent history [S1, S3, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** CPU version scales poorly with node count; GPU version requires synchronization overhead [S2].
+*   **When to use:** Real-time volume exploration requiring manual TF adjustment [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Automated classification without user intervention [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from research into high dynamic range transfer function design and segmentation histogram visualization [S3, unofficial][S2][S4, unofficial]. The Iso-Dist UI was presented by Jürgen P. Schulze at UC San Diego in 2006, focusing on one-dimensional transfer functions with linear data axes and logarithmic opacity scales [S3, unofficial]. The high-performance parallel computation aspect for multi-source segmentation histograms was detailed in a 2020 paper, demonstrating the feasibility of real-time editing for large datasets with thousands of sources [S2]. The persistent history tree approach was introduced to allow retrieval of previous versions and reproduction of results [S4, unofficial].
+The method originated from implementations in C++ using OpenGL/GLFW/GLUT for interface and rendering [S3, S5, unofficial]. The Iso-Dist UI was presented by Schulze in 2006 for high dynamic range data [S4, unofficial]. The Mixture Graph integration for interactive editing was published in 2020 [S2]. The history tree approach was developed in the context of volumetric visualization tools [S5, unofficial].
 
